{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Normalize the force-bypass surface so `--force[=<reason>]` is the single approved pattern across `transition` and `claim`, consistently across the externally-exposed surfaces (CLI + library) AND the internal module/method naming, and remove the redundant `force_close`.\n\nOperator rulings (2026-08-18): `--force[=<reason>]` is the universal approved pattern — the audit reason is tightly coupled to the force operation, so it rides on `--force`, NOT a separate `--reason`; `force_close` is to be removed; blast radius is limited to `transition` + `claim` (tracker-maintenance / init / gates keep their own `--force` conventions and are out of scope).\n\n## Context / Problem\n`claim` already has the target shape: lib `claim(force: str | None = None)` where the value IS the audit reason (bare CLI `--force` -> \"(no reason given)\", `--force=<reason>` -> that reason). `transition` diverges: lib `transition(force: bool, reason: str, force_close: str | None)` splits the one concept across a bool `force`, a separate `reason`, and a redundant `force_close` str; and the transition CLI additionally lets `--reason` stand in as the force-bypass audit note (transition.py help ~lines 42-46, 59-63) — a coupling `claim` does not have. Internally the single concept \"the force-bypass reason\" is spelled four ways: `force_reason` (CLI `_parse_flags`), `force_close` (lib param), `force_close_reason` (txn.py plumbing + a PERSISTED `status_data` key), and `force_plan_review` (claim_compute).\n\n## Approach\nMake `transition` match `claim`'s canonical shape and unify the internal names.\n- Lib: change `transition` to `force: str | None = None` (the audit reason; None = not forcing; any string incl. \"(no reason given)\" = force, bypassing whichever gate THIS transition hits — start-work OR completion-verify close). REMOVE the `force: bool` and `force_close` params. `reason` remains ONLY for its close_reason role (reason-required non-force closes); it no longer doubles as the force-bypass note.\n- Migration: a legacy `rebar.transition(force_close=\"<r>\")` call WARNs via the sanctioned lib-kind deprecation alias (rebar._deprecations `_KINDS` includes `lib`) and maps to `force=\"<r>\"`; a legacy `force=True` maps to `force=\"(no reason given)\"`.\n- CLI (transition.py): keep the `--force[=<reason>]` shape (already present); the force note comes from `--force=<reason>` else \"(no reason given)\" — DROP the `--reason` fallback for the force note so it matches `claim` (reason stays for close_reason only). Bare `--force` -> \"(no reason given)\" exactly as `claim`.\n- Internal naming: unify the IN-MEMORY spelling of the concept to `force_reason` across transition (`_parse_flags`, `transition_compute`, the txn.py param) and claim (`claim_compute`'s `force_plan_review` -> `force_reason`), with no behavior change. KEEP the PERSISTED `status_data[\"force_close_reason\"]` key name unchanged — it is durable reduced state; renaming it is an event-schema migration and is out of scope. Add a short comment noting the persisted key is intentionally retained.\n- Reducer/schema (reducer/_processors_status.py, schemas/ticket_state.schema.json, types.py) UNCHANGED: historical events (incl. the `force_close_reason` status field) keep reducing to the same state.\n\n## Scope\n- src/rebar/_lib_writes.py — `transition`: `force: str | None`, drop `force: bool` + `force_close`; deprecation-map legacy force_close/force(bool) via _deprecations.\n- src/rebar/_commands/transition.py — map `--force[=<reason>]` -> `force` str; drop the `--reason`-as-force-note fallback; adopt the unified `force_reason` name; update usage/help text.\n- src/rebar/_commands/txn.py — thread the unified `force_reason` in-memory name; RETAIN the persisted `force_close_reason` status key.\n- src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py, close_precheck.py — key the close bypass on `force is not None` (was `force_close`), recording the force reason as the audit note.\n- src/rebar/_commands/claim.py + claim_compute — rename `force_plan_review` -> `force_reason` (internal only); the `claim` lib shape is already correct.\n- src/rebar/_deprecations.py — register the lib `force_close`/`force(bool)` deprecation alias.\n- Guidance/help text for transition + claim IS in scope: src/rebar/_commands/gates.py (~302-303) currently prints TWO spellings in one override hint — `claim --force=\"<reason>\"` AND the decoupled `transition --force --reason=\"<reason>\"`; unify BOTH to `--force[=<reason>]`. Reconcile the txn.py override hints (~554, 602, 618: `--force=\"<reason>\"`) and the transition.py usage/help so every transition/claim guidance string shows only the `--force[=<reason>]` pattern (no `--reason`-as-force-note wording).\n- OUT OF SCOPE: tracker-maintenance, init, and the gates break-glass `--force` conventions for OTHER commands; the MCP surface (sibling scratchy-leprous-galago); the argparse refactor (sibling churlish-fruited-dassie).\n\n## Testing\n- `transition ... --force` (bare) bypasses the gate with note \"(no reason given)\"; `--force=\"x\"` records \"x\"; byte-identical to `claim --force` / `--force=x` (interface tests on BOTH verbs assert the same fallback).\n- `transition ... closed --force` bypasses the completion-verify/signature close gate (same closed-without-signature outcome as today's force-close); `--reason` on a force close is NOT used as the force note (behavior-change test).\n- `rebar.transition(force_close=\"x\")` and `rebar.transition(force=True)` WARN via _deprecations and behave as `force=\"x\"` / `force=\"(no reason given)\"` (unit tests).\n- A historical event carrying `force_close_reason` still reduces to the identical ticket state (reducer fixture test).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `transition` lib param is `force: str | None` (reason-carrying), matching `claim`; `force: bool` and `force_close` are removed (grep shows no live `force_close` / `force: bool` param on `transition`).\n- [ ] Bare `--force` -> \"(no reason given)\" and `--force=<reason>` -> that reason, IDENTICALLY for `claim` and `transition` (interface tests).\n- [ ] The transition CLI no longer uses `--reason` as the force-bypass note; `--reason` feeds only close_reason (behavior-change test).\n- [ ] `force is not None` drives the bypass of BOTH the start-work and completion-verify close gates for `transition` (tests).\n- [ ] Legacy `force_close=` / `force=True` calls WARN via rebar._deprecations and map to the new `force` string (unit tests).\n- [ ] The in-memory force-reason concept is spelled `force_reason` across transition + claim internals; the persisted `force_close_reason` status key is retained (grep + a reducer back-compat test pass).\n- [ ] Every transition/claim guidance string (gates.py override hint, txn.py override hints, transition.py usage/help) shows only the `--force[=<reason>]` pattern; no `transition --force --reason=` or `--reason`-as-force-note wording remains (grep).\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` are green; module-size and complexity ratchets respected.","title":"Normalize the --force[=<reason>] surface across transition + claim (lib/CLI/guidance/internal); remove force_close"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787089809667717001,"uuid":"90e627d4-7200-4934-bb9c-7a49bad4463d"}